{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":19,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.classical_jet_not_door_v","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.classical_jet_not_door_v","kind":"theorem","line":219,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L219-L233","doc":"**Door-v specialization.** The Door-v residual is exactly a bar-even (cosh)\nseed `X` (`mono X = 1 • X`) confronting a target whose `n`-th jet carries the\n`(−1)^n` bar-parity character — the measured `(−1)^m` alternation. No classical\njet tower realizes it: already at `n = 1` the classical character `1` disagrees\nwith `(−1)^1 = −1` (using `1 ≠ −1`, i.e. characteristic `≠ 2`). The `(−1)^m`\nresidual is therefore the ramification covering monodromy, not a classical jet. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.classical_jet_no_alternating","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.classical_jet_no_alternating","kind":"theorem","line":198,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L198-L217","doc":"**No-identity (the geometric core of S1).** A classical jet tower grown from\na fixed-`t`-parity seed (`mono X = ε • X`) cannot reproduce a target tower whose\nmonodromy eigenvalue `σ n` genuinely differs from `ε` at some jet `n` where the\ntarget is nonzero. Over an integral domain the constant character `ε` of the\nclassical tower and any nonconstant character `σ` are incompatible. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.classical_jet_monodromy_constant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.classical_jet_monodromy_constant","kind":"theorem","line":186,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L186-L196","doc":"**(ii) Constant monodromy eigenvalue.** If the seed `X` is a fixed-`t`-parity\nstate — an eigenvector of the covering monodromy, `mono X = ε • X` (`ε = 1`\nbar-even / cosh, `ε = −1` bar-odd / sinh) — then EVERY jet `X^{(n)}` has the SAME\nmonodromy eigenvalue `ε`, independent of `n`. The classical prolongation cannot\nmake the eigenvalue depend on the jet index. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.jet_mono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.jet_mono","kind":"theorem","line":179,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L179-L184","doc":"The whole jet tower commutes with the monodromy: `mono` of the `n`-th jet is\nthe `n`-th jet of `mono` (induction on `ddz_comm_mono`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.jet_smul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.jet_smul","kind":"theorem","line":173,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L173-L177","doc":"Homogeneity propagates through the jet tower. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.ddz_smul","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.ddz_smul","kind":"theorem","line":167,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L167-L171","doc":"`d/dz` is `R`-homogeneous (the weight is a fixed scalar function). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.jet_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.jet_succ","kind":"theorem","line":165,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L165-L165","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.jet_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.jet_zero","kind":"theorem","line":163,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L163-L163","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.jet","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.jet","kind":"def","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L157-L161","doc":"The `n`-th classical jet: `n`-fold application of `d/dz` (`X^{(n)} = dⁿX/dzⁿ`\nread on the branch chart). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.ddz_shift_preserves_parity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.ddz_shift_preserves_parity","kind":"theorem","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L149-L153","doc":"The two exponents related by the classical shift share `t`-parity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.ddz_monomial_support","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.ddz_monomial_support","kind":"theorem","line":144,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L144-L147","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.monomialCoeff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.monomialCoeff","kind":"def","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L139-L142","doc":"Concrete `−2` support shift: a monomial `t^k` (`delta k`) maps under `d/dz`\nto a monomial supported at exponent `k − 2` — off `{k-2}` the image vanishes.\nSince `k` and `k − 2` share parity, this is the monomial witness of (i). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.ddz_comm_mono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.ddz_comm_mono","kind":"theorem","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L134-L137","doc":"The classical prolongation commutes with the covering monodromy: it never\nmixes the bar-even and bar-odd sectors. This is statement **(i)** for `d/dz`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.ddz","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.ddz","kind":"def","line":128,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L128-L132","doc":"The **classical Seiberg–Witten jet-prolongation** `d/dz` on the branch chart,\npresented as a degree-`(−2)` shift. The nonzero integer weight `(e+2)` stands in\nfor the geometric `(e+2)/2`; only the exponent shift (support / parity) is\nload-bearing below, and it is weight-free. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.shift2_comm_mono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.shift2_comm_mono","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L118-L126","doc":"**(i) `d/dz` preserves `t`-parity — module-level form.** Any degree-`(−2)`\nshift COMMUTES with the monodromy involution `mono`, hence preserves the `ℤ/2`\ngrading into `mono`-eigenspaces. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.even_shift_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.even_shift_two","kind":"theorem","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L111-L116","doc":"Even/odd exponents are preserved by the `−2` shift: `Even (e + 2) ↔ Even e`.\nThis is the arithmetic heart of \"`d/dz` preserves `t`-parity\". ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.shift2","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.shift2","kind":"def","line":104,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L104-L109","doc":"A degree-`(−2)` exponent shift with arbitrary coefficient weight `c`: the\ncoefficient of `t^e` is read from `t^{e+2}`. The classical geometric prolongation\n`d/dz = (1/(2t)) d/dt` is the member with weight `c(e) = (e+2)/2` (`ddz` below);\nthe parity structure proved here is **weight-independent**, which is exactly why\nthe immaterial `½` scalar can be dropped. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.mono_involutive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.mono_involutive","kind":"theorem","line":99,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L99-L102","doc":"The monodromy is an involution (`(t ↦ −t)² = id`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.mono","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.RamificationParityMonodromy.mono","kind":"def","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/RamificationParityMonodromy.lean#L92-L97","doc":"**Ramification covering monodromy `t ↦ −t`.** On the coefficient model\n`Σ_e f(e) t^e`, substituting `t ↦ −t` sends the coefficient at exponent `e` to\n`(−1)^e · f(e)`: it fixes even-`t`-degree terms and negates odd ones. This is the\n`ℤ/2` grading whose `+1`/`−1` eigenspaces are the bar-even (cosh) and bar-odd\n(sinh) sectors. ","signatureChunk":0}]}